## Why This Is Asked

Tests real-world ability to build autonomous, tool-using planning with LangChain, handling failures and idempotence under latency.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain Tools orchestration
- Planning/Autogen style decomposition
- Idempotence and retry semantics
- Observability and testing strategy

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal pseudoplanner
async function planIncident(incident, tools){
  const steps = [];
  if (incident.severity>3) steps.push({name:'fetchMetrics', tool:'metrics_api'});
  steps.push({name:'lookupRunbook', tool:'runbooks_db'});
  steps.push({name:'ensureTicket', tool:'ticket_api'});
  return steps;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test decision paths across edge cases?
- How would you extend to parallel tool execution when safe?

## Why This Question Matters
Provides a practical, beginner-friendly approach to assessing grounding, code fidelity, and relevance for a developer assistant across private documentation corpora with versioning support.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding responses to exact doc version and section
- Detection of code block hallucinations
- Metrics: precision@1, grounding coverage, and citation drift

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal runner skeleton
const prompts = loadPrompts('prompts-50.json');
prompts.forEach(p => {
  const ans = model.ask(p);
  const grounded = verifyGrounding(ans, p.docVersion, p.section);
  const hallucinated = detectHallucinations(ans, p.docVersion);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    prompt: p.question,
    grounded,
    hallucinated,
    precisionAt1: computePrecision(ans, p.expectedEndpoints)
  }));
});
```
